<Base title="AI Prompt Builder" description="Build a clear, structured AI prompt from your goal, context, audience, constraints, and output format. Free, private, and entirely in your browser." breadcrumbs={[{name:'Free Tools',url:'/tools/'},{name:'AI Prompt Builder',url:'/tools/ai-prompt-builder/'}]}>
<header class="page-heading"><p class="eyebrow mint">/ tools / compose</p><h1>AI Prompt Builder</h1><p>Give your idea a little structure. This tool combines your inputs into a prompt; it does not call an AI model.</p></header>
<noscript><p>This tool needs JavaScript. You can also use the <a href="/prompts/a-practical-ai-prompt-framework/">written prompt framework</a>.</p></noscript>
<div class="tool-workspace"><form id="prompt-form" class="tool-form"><fieldset disabled aria-label="Prompt inputs">
<p class="tool-example-lede" id="example-description">New to prompts? Load a short fictional example to see what each field does. Your own writing will never be overwritten.</p>
<p><button id="load-prompt-example" type="button" aria-describedby="example-description">Try a fictional example</button></p>
<label for="goal">Your goal <span class="mint">(required)</span><textarea id="goal" name="goal" required maxlength="10000" rows="3" placeholder="Explain how to plan a small community event."></textarea></label><label for="context">Context<textarea id="context" name="context" rows="3" maxlength="20000" placeholder="A volunteer team of four, a two-week timeline, and a small budget."></textarea></label><label for="audience">Target audience<input id="audience" name="audience" maxlength="2000" placeholder="First-time event organizers" /></label><label for="constraints">Constraints<textarea id="constraints" name="constraints" rows="2" maxlength="10000" placeholder="Use plain English. Flag missing details. Do not invent costs."></textarea></label><label for="format">Preferred output format<input id="format" name="format" maxlength="2000" placeholder="A numbered plan with a short checklist" /></label><div class="tool-actions"><button class="primary" type="submit">Build my prompt →</button><button type="reset">Reset</button></div><p id="prompt-status" class="tool-status" role="status" aria-live="polite"></p></fieldset></form>
<section class="tool-output" aria-labelledby="output-title"><h2 id="output-title">Your structured prompt</h2><label class="small muted" for="prompt-output">After building, edit the draft directly here. Copy and Download TXT use your final edited wording.</label><textarea id="prompt-output" disabled placeholder="Your prompt will appear here after you select Build my prompt." aria-describedby="prompt-edit-hint"></textarea><p id="prompt-edit-hint" class="tool-footnote">If you change any source field after building, the old draft stays visible for reference but its export buttons are disabled. Build again to generate a current version. If you edited the draft, rebuilding asks before replacing it. Reset clears both the fields and the draft after you confirm; cancelling keeps your work.</p><div class="tool-actions"><button id="copy-prompt" type="button" disabled>Copy</button><button id="download-prompt" type="button" disabled>Download TXT</button></div><p id="copy-status" role="status" class="tool-status"></p><p class="tool-footnote">Inputs stay on this page and are cleared when you reload or reset. Avoid including sensitive information. <a href="/privacy-policy/">Privacy details</a></p></section></div>
<section class="section prose info-content">
<h2>How to use each field without inventing facts</h2>
<p>The builder is a <strong>local text formatter</strong>, not an AI assistant. It turns five optional or required fields into a reusable instruction you can inspect and edit. It cannot know whether your context is correct or whether a future AI answer follows the instructions. Before copying the result, look for these distinct roles:</p>
<table><thead><tr><th>Field</th><th>What to enter</th><th>What to avoid</th></tr></thead><tbody>
<tr><td>Goal</td><td>One observable output, such as “draft a meeting invitation”</td><td>“Do something helpful” without an outcome</td></tr>
<tr><td>Context</td><td>Confirmed facts the assistant may use</td><td>Putting a guess next to an approved fact without labeling it</td></tr>
<tr><td>Audience</td><td>Who will read or use the result</td><td>Invented demographics or customer research</td></tr>
<tr><td>Constraints</td><td>Boundaries you can inspect afterward</td><td>Uncheckable demands such as “make it perfect”</td></tr>
<tr><td>Format</td><td>A practical shape, length, or required sections</td><td>Combining conflicting output formats</td></tr>
</tbody></table>
<h2>Worked exercise: keep an unknown location unknown</h2>
<p>Select <strong>Try a fictional example</strong> while the form is empty, then select <strong>Build my prompt</strong>. The example is based on the <a href="/examples/claim-boundary-source-notes.txt">publicly available fictional garden notes</a>: the meeting is on Saturday at 10 a.m., attendees should bring a notebook, and the group will choose a planting schedule. <strong>No meeting location is confirmed.</strong> All these details were invented for teaching; we did not collect them from a real event organizer.</p>
<p>Check the generated prompt against four conditions before you use it in another application:</p>
<ol>
<li><strong>Purpose:</strong> it requests an invitation, not a confirmed booking or an event review.</li>
<li><strong>Source boundary:</strong> it includes Saturday at 10 a.m. and the notebook instruction, but no named venue.</li>
<li><strong>Explicit uncertainty:</strong> the constraint asks for <code>[TO CONFIRM]</code> rather than inventing a location or organizer.</li>
<li><strong>Inspectable format:</strong> the request asks for a friendly invitation below 70 words. Whether a later AI response respects this is a separate check.</li>
</ol>
<p>The output is an instruction <em>for</em> another tool, not an answer produced <em>by</em> a model. You must still inspect any resulting invitation. For example, “Meet us in the North Greenhouse” should be rejected unless the organizer separately confirms that location.</p>
<h2>A small failure-injection test you can do yourself</h2>
<p>Make one change at a time to see how the builder behaves. This checks the local formatter rather than claiming improved model accuracy.</p>
<ol>
<li><strong>Clear the goal:</strong> reset the form and fill only the Context field, then submit. The required Goal field should prevent a meaningful prompt from being built.</li>
<li><strong>Remove the uncertainty rule:</strong> load the example, then delete “Do not invent a location” from Constraints. The generated prompt loses that explicit safeguard; the builder cannot infer that it should be restored.</li>
<li><strong>Keep an empty optional field:</strong> omit Audience or Format. The builder should still produce usable text using the supplied fields, without fabricating an audience or format.</li>
<li><strong>Protect your own writing:</strong> enter a goal and click the example button. The example should not overwrite the value already entered; select Reset first if you want the fictional inputs.</li>
</ol>
<h2>Verify that edits are exported—not silently discarded</h2>
<p>Use the fictional sample to build a prompt. In the generated draft, change <code>under 70 words</code> to <code>under 50 words</code>, then choose Copy or Download TXT. Both actions use the text currently shown in the editable draft—not an earlier hidden version. Now alter the original Audience field: the previous draft stays visible, but export is disabled until you rebuild it. This prevents an outdated instruction from being passed along accidentally.</p>
<p>If you need to preserve your edited version before changing the source fields, copy or download it first. Rebuilding the prompt replaces the current draft with a new one based on the updated fields, but you must confirm first if you edited the draft. Reset also asks before clearing nonempty fields or drafts. Cancelling either action keeps the current wording. Nothing is sent to GuideHollow or an external AI service.</p>
<p><strong>Independent UI evidence:</strong> we ran this edit-and-rebuild sequence on the public GuideHollow website on November 1, 2026. The results and the limitation of the clipboard confirmation are recorded in the <a href="/evidence/">Evidence Library</a> and the <a href="/examples/firsthand-browser-qa-2026-11-01.json">structured browser log</a>. This is evidence about our own browser tool, not about another AI product's answers.</p>
<h2>What a successful prompt does not prove</h2>
<p>Clear instructions reduce ambiguity but do not establish factual truth, privacy compliance, or the reliability of a particular model. Never paste private company records, passwords, patient information, or secret tokens without permission. The builder does not transmit the text to our server, but <strong>copying and pasting it into an external AI service is a separate disclosure</strong> governed by that service's controls.</p>
<p>For a different task, see the <a href="/prompts/how-to-write-better-ai-prompts/">bounded prompting guide</a>. After receiving an answer, try the <a href="/tools/ai-response-checklist/">AI Response Quality Checklist</a>, or use the <a href="/tools/claim-checker/">Source Claim Verifier</a> to practice distinguishing contradicted facts from missing information. The original exercise data and limits are documented in the <a href="/evidence/">Evidence Library</a>.</p>
</section>
